<!-- ================= PERMISSION ================= -->
<section class="band band--wash" id="permission">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="col">
      <p class="eyebrow rise">The question you already searched</p>
      <h2 class="wide rise">Is this allowed — and is my prayer worth less?</h2>
      <p class="rise">Most people search this before they search for anything to buy. We're not scholars and won't pretend to be. Here's what the texts say, <strong>with the references</strong>, so you can check them yourself and take them to someone qualified.</p>

      <h3 class="rise">On praying seated when you can't do otherwise</h3>
      <p class="rise">The Prophet ﷺ told 'Imran ibn Husayn: pray standing; if you cannot, then sitting; and if you cannot, then on your side — <em>Sahih al-Bukhari 1066</em>. Ibn Qudamah records in <em>al-Mughni</em> (1/443) that the scholars are unanimous that a person unable to stand may pray sitting. And Allah does not burden a soul beyond its capacity (2:286), and asks that we keep our duty to Him as much as we are able (64:16).</p>

      <h3 class="rise">On the half the reward narration</h3>
      <p class="rise">There is an authentic narration that one who prays sitting receives <strong>half the reward</strong> of one who stands — <em>Sahih al-Bukhari 1115–1116</em>. If you know it, it's probably the reason you haven't bought anything yet.</p>
      <p class="rise">Read what the scholars did with it.</p>

      <div class="note rise">
        <p><strong>Al-Nawawi</strong>, in <em>al-Majmu'</em> (4/226), states that the ummah is agreed that whoever is genuinely unable to stand in an obligatory prayer may pray sitting, does not repeat it, and <strong><mark>his reward is not reduced, because he is excused.</mark></strong> He grounds this in a second authentic narration in al-Bukhari: when a person is sick or travelling, there is written for him the reward of what he used to do when he was healthy and at home.</p>
        <p><strong>Al-Khattabi</strong>, quoted approvingly by Ibn Hajar in <em>Fath al-Bari</em> (2/468), reads the halving the same way: it addresses the sick person who is <em>still able</em> to stand with difficulty. The reward is halved to encourage him to stand — while still permitting him to sit.</p>
      </div>
      <p class="src rise" style="margin-top:14px">So there are two narrations, not one. The first is about the person who could stand and chose not to. The second is about you.</p>

      <h3 class="rise">Two things worth knowing about this stool in particular</h3>
      <p class="rise"><strong>Your sujud stays on the ground.</strong> Nothing is placed under your forehead — which matters, because there is a narration in which the Prophet ﷺ removed a pillow a sick man had put down to prostrate on. Some prayer chairs are sold with a shelf or tabletop for exactly that purpose. This has nothing of the kind, by design.</p>
      <p class="rise"><strong>And you stay low.</strong> Every authority we read on accommodation — across more than one madhhab — preferred that the worshipper remain close to the ground rather than be raised up onto furniture. 25cm is a great deal closer to the ground than a chair is.</p>

      <div class="note rise">
        <h3 style="margin-top:0">Now do the thing we would do</h3>
        <p>Take this page, or the references on it, to your own imam and ask him about your own case. He knows your madhhab and he knows you. We don't, and it would be wrong of us to answer as though we did.</p>
        <p><a href="/blogs/journal/is-it-allowed-to-pray-sitting-down" style="color:var(--gold-a)">The longer write-up, with all the references in one place →</a></p>
        <p style="margin-bottom:0"><em>We'll never pretend to be scholars. We just make the seat.</em></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= THE 25CM RULE — SIGNATURE ================= -->
<section class="band">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="col">
      <p class="eyebrow rise">Why 25cm is the whole design</p>
      <h2 class="wide rise">Measure it against the floor.</h2>
      <p class="rise">A dining chair is around 45cm. Sit on one in the masjid and you're <strong>half a metre above the row</strong> — everyone sees it. That's why you haven't used one.</p>
      <p class="rise">Now measure the other way. Sitting on your heels, your hips are already about <strong>19cm off the floor</strong>. The Sajda Stool is <strong>25</strong>.</p>
      <p class="rise"><mark>You end up 6cm higher than you already were.</mark> Nobody beside you registers the difference.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="rulewrap">
      <div class="rule rise" role="img" aria-label="Height comparison: sitting on your heels is about 19cm, the Sajda stool is 25cm, and a dining chair is about 45cm — so the stool is only about 6cm higher than sitting on your heels.">
