<!-- ================= THE MAT ================= -->
<section class="band band--white">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="col">
      <p class="eyebrow rise">If you already tried something</p>
      <h2 class="wide rise">You bought the thick mat. It didn't fix this.</h2>
      <p class="rise">Memory foam, orthopaedic, the expensive one your daughter found. It helped a little, and you concluded nothing will.</p>
      <p class="rise">A mat changes what the floor feels like. It can't change the angle your knee is folded to. Sitting on your heels closes the joint as far as it goes and loads your whole weight on top. <mark>Nothing you lay on the floor can open that angle.</mark></p>
      <p class="rise">One mat maker admits it on their own website: they keep the foam under an inch where your forehead lands, because sujud has to touch the ground. A prayer mat can only get so thick.</p>
      <p class="rise">You didn't fail at using it. It was built for a different problem.</p>
      <p class="rise"><a href="/blogs/journal/do-thick-prayer-mats-help-knee-pain">More on why thick prayer mats only fix half of it →</a></p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= VS ================= -->
<section class="band">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="col">
      <p class="eyebrow rise">Against the alternative</p>
      <h2 class="wide rise">A prayer chair solves a different problem, for a different person.</h2>
      <p class="rise">If you can't get down to the floor at all, buy a chair. If you can get down and <strong>it's the sitting that defeats you</strong>, you want a much smaller object.</p>
      <p class="rise"><a href="/blogs/journal/prayer-chair-or-prayer-stool">The full comparison: prayer chair or prayer stool →</a></p>
    </div>

    <table class="vs rise">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>&nbsp;</th><th>A prayer chair</th><th class="mine">Sajda</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="attr">Seat height</td><td>Around 45cm</td><td class="mine">25cm</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="attr">Weight</td><td>Around 12kg</td><td class="mine">1.2kg</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="attr">In the row</td><td>Sits above it</td><td class="mine">Level with it</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="attr">Sujud</td><td>Often onto a shelf</td><td class="mine">On the floor</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="attr">Getting it there</td><td>Stays where it's kept</td><td class="mine">Carried in one hand</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="attr">Who it's for</td><td>Someone who prays seated throughout</td><td class="mine">Someone who stands, bows and prostrates — but can't hold the sitting</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= WHO IT ISN'T FOR ================= -->
<section class="band band--ink">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="col">
      <p class="eyebrow rise">Read this before you order</p>
      <h2 class="wide rise">This stool asks you to kneel. For some people that rules it out.</h2>
      <p class="rise">To use it you have to <strong>get down onto your knees</strong>, fold your shins under, and get back up.</p>
      <p class="rise"><strong style="color:#fff">If you can't get down to the floor at all, this is the wrong product.</strong> A stool won't solve that, and we're not going to tell you it will. Buy a proper prayer chair; several are sold in the UK.</p>
      <p class="rise">This is for the person who can still get down and still make sujud, <strong>but can't hold the sitting in between</strong>. If that's you, we don't know of anything else built for it.</p>
      <p class="rise" style="margin-bottom:0">We'd rather lose the order than have it arrive and be wrong for you.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

